WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.295
Lesson 5.3.

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:04.675
Oracles, TWAPs & manipulation.

00:00:04.675 --> 00:00:06.705
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:06.705 --> 00:00:09.064
able to know which price feed secures each

00:00:09.064 --> 00:00:10.710
position, and how it can fail.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.300
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.985
First, the key ideas.

00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:17.700
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.225
Then, your checklist.

00:00:19.225 --> 00:00:21.387
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:22.687 --> 00:00:24.664
Smart contracts can't see market

00:00:24.664 --> 00:00:26.762
prices; oracles bring them in.

00:00:26.762 --> 00:00:29.799
Lending markets liquidate based on the oracle price,

00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:31.932
not the price you see on screen.

00:00:31.932 --> 00:00:35.385
First, Push oracles: a network updates prices on a

00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:39.477
schedule or when price moves past a deviation threshold.

00:00:39.477 --> 00:00:42.170
Next, Pull oracles: prices are fetched and

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.912
verified when a transaction needs them.

00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:48.184
Then, TWAP (time-weighted average price) smooths

00:00:48.184 --> 00:00:50.754
manipulation, but lags fast moves.

00:00:50.754 --> 00:00:54.087
And finally, Failures: stale prices in outages,

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:57.559
manipulation of thin markets, misconfigured feeds.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:01.374
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:01.374 --> 00:01:04.329
Your collateral is priced by a 30-minute TWAP.

00:01:04.329 --> 00:01:07.099
The market drops 10% in 5 minutes.

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:12.204
Your screen shows −10%; the oracle shows maybe −2%.

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:15.175
You feel liquidated and aren't yet, but you also

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:17.719
can't rely on the lag: it catches up.

00:01:17.719 --> 00:01:20.988
Conversely, a thinly traded collateral token can be

00:01:20.988 --> 00:01:23.879
pushed up and borrowed against by an attacker.

00:01:25.179 --> 00:01:26.639
Here's your checklist.

00:01:26.639 --> 00:01:28.174
Do it now, for real.

00:01:28.174 --> 00:01:31.889
First, Oracle type and source known for each collateral.

00:01:31.889 --> 00:01:35.043
Next, Thin-market collateral avoided or capped.

00:01:35.043 --> 00:01:37.958
And finally, Buffers sized for oracle catch-up.

00:01:39.258 --> 00:01:40.468
Question 1.

00:01:40.468 --> 00:01:42.753
Which price triggers a DeFi liquidation?

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:50.398
The answer: The oracle price used by the protocol.

00:01:51.698 --> 00:01:52.943
Question 2.

00:01:52.943 --> 00:01:54.648
What's the trade-off of a TWAP?

00:01:59.068 --> 00:02:01.142
The answer: Harder to manipulate,

00:02:01.142 --> 00:02:02.545
but it lags fast moves.

00:02:03.845 --> 00:02:05.115
Question 3.

00:02:05.115 --> 00:02:08.000
Why is thin-market collateral dangerous for lenders?

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.711
The answer: Its price can be manipulated

00:02:14.711 --> 00:02:16.835
to borrow more than it's really worth.

00:02:18.135 --> 00:02:19.380
Let's recap.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.910
Push oracles: a network updates prices on a schedule

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:26.340
or when price moves past a deviation threshold.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.558
Pull oracles: prices are fetched and

00:02:28.558 --> 00:02:31.195
verified when a transaction needs them.

00:02:31.195 --> 00:02:34.149
TWAP (time-weighted average price) smooths

00:02:34.149 --> 00:02:36.785
manipulation, but lags fast moves.

00:02:36.785 --> 00:02:40.139
Failures: stale prices in outages, manipulation

00:02:40.139 --> 00:02:42.725
of thin markets, misconfigured feeds.

00:02:44.025 --> 00:02:46.540
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.839
Next up, Lesson 5.4: Smart

00:02:48.839 --> 00:02:52.075
contracts: state, proxies, admin keys.
